  description="GameBoyGhost, in plain English: teaching a computer to play Link's Awakening, one stretch at a time, under real change control."
>
  <h1>What it is</h1>

  <p class="lede">
    GameBoyGhost teaches a computer program to play <em>The Legend of Zelda: Link's Awakening</em>,
    one short stretch at a time, by copying an expert.
  </p>

  <p>
    The game is a safe practice target. The real point is to show how to run a technical system
    carefully and honestly: every run can be repeated exactly, every result is checked before it
    counts, and every mistake gets written up instead of hidden.
  </p>

  <blockquote>Imagine wanting to practice good IT habits — change control, audit trails, sign-offs, honest postmortems. You don't practice on a real production system. You use a test system that behaves the same way every time. A Game Boy game in an emulator is exactly that.</blockquote>

  <h2>How it works, in three pieces</h2>

  <p>
    The game's journey is split into <strong>segments</strong> — short stretches with a clear start
    and finish. For each one, the project builds three things:
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>
      A <strong>teacher</strong> (later, an <strong>oracle</strong>) — an expert program that already
      knows how to play that stretch.
    </li>
    <li>
      A <strong>student</strong> — a small learned program that copies the expert's answers, seeing
      only a limited view of the game. This is imitation learning.
    </li>
    <li>
      A <strong>gate</strong> — a test written down in advance and run exactly once, that decides
      whether the segment is done and sealed. Failures are never re-run with an easier bar.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Why a game makes a good test system</h2>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>It's repeatable.</strong> Every run is its own process; re-runs match byte for byte.</li>
    <li><strong>It's self-contained.</strong> No network, no clock, one fixed start, button presses only.</li>
    <li><strong>You can see everything.</strong> "Did this work?" is an exact yes-or-no, not a judgment call.</li>
    <li><strong>There's a real expert.</strong> The route was mapped room by room, the way a seasoned admin documents a system.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    The deeper version — the exact numbers, the gate math, the full architecture — lives in
    <a href="/pitch/">the engine room</a>. This page is the plain-English version; nothing here is
    simplified at the expense of what's true.
  </p>
</Layout>
